# death_if_fails rescaled for real per-act risk (drafts/repeats/rescale_death.py)
# Chroma library, modern Earth: TITLE STAGES of eight common titles (next batch, Emren's point 6, 2026-10-05): for each
# title three moments only its holder meets, by years held (tenure: 0-1 new, 2-7 settled, 8-60 senior or veteran):
# scout or guide, on the team, shop assistant, waiter or bartender, office clerk, care worker, soldier, teacher.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: library-spec.md, writer-brief.md, drafts/next2/BRIEF.md. Catalogue: drafts/next2/earth_perks_titles.py.
# Every moment: tier everyday, calls two colors (spread over the file: W10 U10 B9 R9 G10), the five one-color options and
# one cross cycle (s1 to s4, six each). Options open the titles' real paths (title:, grants:, drops:) from the catalogue.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- scout or guide, new (tenure 0-1): calls WG (cycle s1)

== the promise at the first camp
tier: everyday
stages: child juvenile
age: 6-15
calls: WG
holds: scout or guide
tenure: 0-1
rate: 2
once: yes
tone: joy
life: community, nature
horizon: week
roles: mentor, friend, parent, the other scouts
earth: on your first weekend camp with the troop you make the scout or guide promise by the fire and are given your neckerchief
times: Nearly every new scout or guide, once, at the first camp, usually at 6 to 12; scouting and guiding count tens of millions of young members worldwide (WOSM, WAGGGS)
scene: It is {Ns} first camp with the troop: a wet field, six tents and a fire that took an hour to light. Tonight, in front of everyone, {N} will make the promise and be given the neckerchief.
scene W: {N} has practised the promise all week in front of the bathroom mirror. Every word of it is going to be said right, and meant.
scene U: The tent poles came in a bag with no instructions, and {N} worked out which went where before anyone else. There are forty knots in the handbook still to learn.
scene B: The older scouts have the best pitch, by the stream, and they get to light the fire. {N} watches who hands out the jobs, and how to get the good ones.
scene R: Mud on the knees, smoke in the hair, a torch under the sleeping bag and nobody's parent for miles. {N} has never felt so awake.
scene G: Away from the town lights the stars come out one by one, and the wood behind the tents is full of small sounds. {N} lies outside the tent, listening.
- W | say the promise word for word in front of everyone, and mean every line | identity | binds | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 75
- U | learn every knot in the handbook before the weekend is out | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 75
- B | trade your sweets for a turn at lighting the fire with the older ones | v: power, hedonism | chance: 88
- R | creep out after lights out with a torch to explore the wood | v: stimulation | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: out of the tent after lights out; backfire: caught, and sent home a day early | chance: 65
- G | lie outside the tent half the night and learn the sounds of the wood | door | v: universalism (nature) | grants: knowing the woods | chance: 70
- W>U | ask the leader what each line of the promise really means | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 85
- U>B | study the badge chart and start with the badges that come quickest | v: achievement | chance: 85
- B>R | bet the older ones you can jump the stream where it is widest | body: heavy | v: stimulation, power | chance: 65
- R>G | sing the camp songs loudest of all round the fire | v: tradition, hedonism | chance: 92
- G>W | light the fire the way the old leader says the troop always has | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 80
works: The neckerchief goes round {Ns} neck, the troop cheers, and the woggle slides up snug.
works: {N} comes home smelling of woodsmoke and talks about the camp for a week.
fails: Rain gets into the tent on the first night, and {N} spends the second day cold and miserable.
fails: {N} stumbles over the words in front of everyone, and the older ones snigger.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- scout or guide, settled (tenure 2-7): calls WU (cycle s2)

== the patrol chooses a new leader
tier: everyday
stages: child juvenile young_adult
age: 10-18
calls: WU
holds: scout or guide
tenure: 2-7
rate: 1
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: community, friends
horizon: months
roles: rival, friend, mentor, the younger scouts
earth: your patrol needs a new leader, and you could stand for it, or go for the hardest badge in the troop instead
times: Many scouts and guides who stay a few years, once, in the early teens; each patrol picks a new leader every year or two (estimate)
scene: The patrol leader has moved up to the older section, and the patrol has to choose who takes the cord next. {N} has been in the troop for three years, and so has {rival}.
scene W: A patrol leader keeps the patrol box in order, gets the young ones to camp on time and answers to the leaders for all of them. {N} knows it is a real job.
scene U: There is also the expedition badge, which nobody in the patrol has managed: a route, a map, a night out and a written log. {N} has been reading up on it for months.
scene B: The cord comes with the say over who cooks, who washes up and who gets the good tent. {N} counts the votes in their head.
scene R: {N} would love to lead the patrol on their own wild ideas, and hates the thought of {rival} bossing everyone about.
scene G: The patrol has been together for years, like a small family in muddy shorts. {N} wonders who it would really want.
- W | stand for leader, and promise the leaders to see it through | identity | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 60
- U | go for the expedition badge instead, and plan the route all winter | v: achievement, self-direction | grants: navigation | self_control: + | chance: 60
- B | talk to every younger one in the patrol before the vote, one by one | v: power, achievement | chance: 70
- R | tell the patrol you would lead it on the wildest camp they have ever had | v: stimulation | chance: 65
- G | stay out of it, and back whoever the patrol takes to | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 70
- W>B | run the patrol's kit check every week, so the leaders see who is ready | habit | v: achievement, power | chance: 80
- U>R | show what you would do: plan a night hike on a route of your own | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 70
- B>G | back the quiet one who knows the woods, and be the one they lean on | v: power, benevolence | chance: 75
- R>W | stand up at the meeting and demand a proper secret ballot | v: universalism | chance: 72
- G>U | find the old leader who ran the patrol years ago, and ask their advice | door | v: tradition, self-direction | grants: mentor | chance: 70
works: By the end of term the patrol runs better than it ever has, and {N} has something new on the sleeve to show for it.
works: {mentor} takes {N} aside after the meeting and says the troop needs more like {N}.
fails: The patrol splits over it, and the next hike is a misery from start to finish.
fails: Nothing goes {Ns} way, and {N} sulks through three Tuesday meetings in a row.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- scout or guide, senior (tenure 8-60): calls WG (cycle s3)

== the oldest one at the campfire
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile young_adult
age: 14-18
calls: WG
holds: scout or guide
tenure: 8-60
rate: .5
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: community, nature
horizon: months
roles: mentor, friend, parent, the younger scouts
earth: after eight years in the troop you are the oldest at camp, the leaders ask you to help run things, and your own friends have stopped coming
times: Few: perhaps 1 in 10 of those who join scouts or guides, once, after eight years, at 14 to 18; most leave by their early teens (estimate)
scene: Eight summers on, {N} is the oldest at camp. The leaders have asked {N} to help run the week for the young ones, and most of {Ns} own year has drifted off to other things.
scene W: The leaders trust {N} with the headcount, the first-aid box and the fire. There are twenty young ones, and every one of them has to go home in one piece.
scene U: {N} knows every knot, the summer stars and how to read a contour line, and the young ones keep getting wrong the things {N} could easily teach.
scene B: Helping to run a camp of twenty would look good on any application. {N} also notices that the leaders now ask, and no longer tell.
scene R: {Ns} friends are at a festival this week. {N} sits by the fire with a ring of eleven-year-olds and wonders, for a moment, what on earth {N} is doing here.
scene G: {mentor} has run this camp for forty years, in the same field by the same river. By the fire one night {mentor} says it will soon need someone else.
- W | do the headcounts, the rotas and the safety checks by the book, all week | v: security, conformity | grants: organising people | chance: 80
- U | teach the young ones map and compass, one patrol at a time | v: benevolence, achievement | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 80
- B | put running the camp on every application, and ask the leaders for a reference | v: achievement | chance: 80
- R | tell the leaders this is your last camp, and spend next summer with your friends | v: self-direction, hedonism | drops: scout or guide | chance: 85
- G | promise the old leader to come back one day and help run this camp | binds | v: tradition | mark: kept your word | chance: 80
- W>R | sign up for the young leaders' training, to run camps of your own one day | door | v: benevolence, stimulation | grants: minding small children | chance: 75
- U>G | show the young ones how to light a fire with no matches, and leave no trace | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 70
- B>W | take charge of the camp tuck shop and its money, and keep the books straight | habit | v: achievement, security | grants: bookkeeping | chance: 85
- R>U | climb the hill alone before dawn to watch the sun come up over the camp | body: light | v: stimulation | chance: 80
- G>B | lead the youngest patrol to the woodcraft trophy on what you know of the woods | v: achievement, power | chance: 60
works: The young ones go home talking about the week, and three of them ask whether {N} will be there next year.
works: {mentor} shakes {Ns} hand on the last morning, the way one leader shakes another's.
fails: A young one goes missing for an hour on {Ns} watch, and the week ends under a cloud.
fails: The week drags, and {N} spends most of it wishing to be somewhere else.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- on the team, new (tenure 0-1): calls UR (cycle s4)

== the first match in the club shirt
tier: everyday
stages: child juvenile
age: 7-16
calls: UR
holds: on the team
tenure: 0-1
rate: 2
once: yes
tone: joy
life: sport, friends
horizon: moment
roles: mentor, parent, friend, the other players
earth: your first real match for the club, in a shirt that is still too big, starting on the bench
times: Nearly every child who joins a sports club, once, in the first season; about half of US children aged 6 to 17 play on a team or take sport lessons (NSCH)
scene: The shirt is two sizes too big and the number on the back is fourteen. {N} starts on the bench in the drizzle, waiting for {mentor} to call the name.
scene W: {mentor} told {N} to hold the left side and not to wander. {N} intends to stay exactly there, all game, whatever happens.
scene U: From the bench {N} watches the other side's tall striker and sees that he always turns the same way. Somebody should tell the defenders.
scene B: The parents on the touchline shout the names of the players they rate. {N} wants to be one of those names by the end of the season.
scene R: {N} cannot keep still on the bench. The ball, the mud, the noise: {N} wants to be out there so much it aches.
scene G: {parent} played for this club once; there is an old team photograph in the clubhouse with the family name under it. {N} pulls the shirt straight.
- W | when sent on, hold the position the coach gave you and nothing else | v: conformity | chance: 75
- U | tell the defenders at half-time which way the striker always turns | v: achievement, benevolence | chance: 75
- B | practise alone after every session until the starting place is your own | habit | v: achievement | self_control: + | chance: 75
- R | chase every ball from the moment you go on, until the legs give out | body: heavy | v: stimulation | chance: 80
- G | forget the team talk and play on instinct, the way you play in the park | v: self-direction, hedonism | closed: approval: ignoring the coach's instructions; backfire: taken off at once, and left on the bench the next week | chance: 65
- W>G | help carry the nets and the kit bags back to the clubhouse afterwards | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 80
- U>W | learn the offside rule properly before next Saturday | v: conformity, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 85
- B>U | ask the best player in the team to show you their tricks after training | v: achievement | act: ask the best player in the team to show them some tricks after training | chance: 75
- R>B | go in hard on the biggest player on the other side, to show no fear | body: heavy | v: power | chance: 65
- G>R | stop worrying about the score, and just enjoy being out there in the mud | v: hedonism | chance: 85
works: {mentor} sends {N} on with ten minutes left, and {N} wins the ball in a way that makes the parents cheer.
works: After the match the team piles into the clubhouse for squash and crisps, and somebody calls {N} by a nickname for the first time.
fails: {N} never gets off the bench, and goes home cold and quiet.
fails: {N} goes on, slips in the mud at the first touch, and the other side scores from it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- on the team, settled (tenure 2-7): calls BG (cycle s1)

== the coach asks who wants the armband
tier: everyday
stages: child juvenile young_adult
age: 9-18
calls: BG
holds: on the team
tenure: 2-7
rate: 1
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: sport, friends
horizon: months
roles: mentor, friend, rival, parent
earth: the captain is moving away and the coach asks who wants the armband, the same week a bigger club comes to watch you play
times: Some young players, perhaps 1 in 5, once, a few years into a team; one captain to a squad of fifteen or so (estimate)
scene: The captain is moving away, and at the end of training {mentor} asks who wants the armband. The same week, a coach from the big club across town has been seen on the touchline, watching {N}.
scene W: A captain is first at training, last to leave, and speaks to the referee for the team. {N} has been turning up for three years and knows how it should be done.
scene U: The big club plays a quicker game, with set moves drilled all week. {N} wants to know what it would teach.
scene B: The armband means the say over penalties and the line-up talk; the big club means better kit, better matches and maybe a future. {N} weighs them both.
scene R: {N} loves this team: the mud, the jokes in the minibus, the songs on the way home. The thought of leaving it hurts, and so does the thought of never finding out.
scene G: The team has grown up together since they were small. {friend} has been at {Ns} side on the pitch since the very first season.
- W | put your hand up for the armband, and be first to every session | binds | v: conformity, benevolence | title: team captain | chance: 60
- U | ask for a trial at the big club, to see how they train | door | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 70
- B | take the trial, train twice a day for it, and go if they want you | identity | v: achievement, power | self_control: + | chance: 70
- R | stay with the team you love, and tell the big club no to their face | identity | v: hedonism, benevolence | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 80
- G | tell the coach your friend should be captain, the one who holds everyone together | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 65
- W>U | ask the coach what a captain needs to learn, and work on it all summer | v: achievement | chance: 72
- U>B | study the big club's matches, and show at the trial exactly what they lack | v: achievement | chance: 60
- B>R | play the trial for yourself, not the team: dribble, shoot, show off | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 60
- R>G | get the whole team to the park to choose the captain themselves | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 65
- G>W | give it a season, as everyone does, and let the coach decide | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 70
works: By the next match {N} knows where {N} belongs on the pitch, and plays like it.
works: {mentor} claps {N} on the shoulder in front of the whole team.
fails: Nothing goes to plan, and {N} spends the next month on the bench.
fails: The team takes it badly, and the minibus goes quiet when {N} gets on.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- on the team, senior (tenure 8-60): calls UG (cycle s2)

== one more season, or the last
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 16-60
calls: UG
holds: on the team
tenure: 8-60
rate: .5
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: sport, body
horizon: years
roles: mentor, rival, friend, partner
earth: after many years on the team, the body or the calendar says this could be your last season
times: Most long-time players, once, between their late teens and their forties; most amateur players stop between their twenties and forties (estimate)
scene: The new season starts in three weeks, and {N} has been on this team longer than almost anyone. Between a knee that took a month to settle after the last knock and everything else the week now asks, {N} has to decide whether to sign the form again.
scene W: The team needs numbers, and {N} has never once let it down on a match day. Signing again would be the dependable thing.
scene U: {N} has read about knees, recovery and what a body can still take. The facts are clear enough; what to do with them is not.
scene B: {rival} is younger, hungrier and wants {Ns} place. {N} is not ready to hand it over for nothing.
scene R: Nothing else in the week feels like the first whistle on a cold morning. {N} cannot imagine the weekends without it.
scene G: Every player comes to a last season in the end. Years ago {N} watched the old ones go the same way, one by one.
- W | sign again, and keep turning up as long as the team needs you | v: conformity, benevolence | act: sign again, and keep turning up as long as the team needs them | mark: kept your word | chance: 75
- U | see the physio, do the exercises every morning, and play one more year | v: achievement | self_control: + | chance: 65
- B | keep your place by outworking the young one all pre-season | body: heavy | v: achievement, power | self_control: + | chance: 65
- R | play every match this season as if it were the last one | body: heavy | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 80
- G | hang up the boots at the end of the season, and watch from the touchline | identity | v: tradition (acceptance) | drops: on the team | chance: 75
- W>B | sit down with the coach and agree a new role: fewer minutes, more say | v: power, security | chance: 70
- U>R | take the referee's course, and stay out on the pitch another way | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | grants: referee's badge | title: sports referee | drops: on the team | chance: 70
- B>G | use your years at the club to get the old crowd a veterans' side | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 70
- R>W | offer to coach the little ones on Sunday mornings, and do the badge | habit | v: benevolence | grants: coaching badge | chance: 70
- G>U | spend the summer walking the hills instead, and see whether the body misses it | v: self-direction, tradition | door | chance: 70
works: The season goes better than {N} feared, and ends with a handshake from everyone in the clubhouse.
works: {N} finds the right shape for the years ahead, and the weekends still have the team in them.
fails: The knee goes again in the fourth match, and the decision is made for {N}.
fails: It turns sour: {N} is on the bench more than off it, and goes home angry most weeks.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- shop assistant, new (tenure 0-1): calls BR (cycle s3)

== a customer shouts at the new face behind the till
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 14-70
calls: BR
holds: shop assistant
tenure: 0-1
rate: 2
once: yes
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: moment
roles: boss, colleague, a customer, the queue
earth: in your first weeks on the tills a customer starts shouting at you over a price, and the whole queue is watching
times: Most new shop workers, once, in the first weeks; about 7 in 10 UK shop workers report verbal abuse in a year (USDAW survey)
scene: It is {Ns} second week on the tills. A customer slams a packet of batteries down, says the shelf said two for one, and starts shouting while the queue behind watches.
scene W: The training said: stay polite, never argue, and call the supervisor for any refund over the limit. {N} reaches for the bell and the rule at the same time.
scene U: Either the shelf label is wrong or the till is. {N} wants to know which, and why nobody has noticed before.
scene B: There is a queue of twelve, a supervisor who hates being called, and a till that will let {N} knock money off. {N} looks for the cheapest way out of this.
scene R: The customer's face is a foot from {Ns} and getting redder. {Ns} own temper is climbing to meet it.
scene G: {N} has seen this before, only from the other side of the till: a tired person at the end of a long day, shouting at whoever is nearest.
- W | ring for the supervisor, as the training says, and wait | v: conformity, security | chance: 85
- U | walk to the shelf with the customer and check the label together | v: universalism (fairness), achievement | chance: 75
- B | say whatever calms them fastest, true or not, and get them out | v: security | chance: 85
- R | tell the customer straight not to speak to you like that | v: self-direction | act: tell the customer straight not to speak to them like that | closed: approval: arguing with a customer; backfire: a complaint and a written warning | chance: 65
- G | let the customer shout it out, and answer softly until it passes | v: benevolence, security | grants: calming people down | chance: 60
- W>R | say firmly that the price is the price, and hold your ground | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 50
- U>G | spot that the next till is free, and wave half the queue over to it | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- B>W | ask the manager to have the customer barred as abusive | v: security, power | mark: made an enemy | chance: 60
- R>U | laugh, say sorry, and ask what they need batteries for at this hour | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 75
- G>B | keep your calm and take it; customers like this one pay the wages | v: security | self_control: + | chance: 80
works: The shouting stops, the queue moves, and the customer even mutters a sorry on the way out.
works: The supervisor hears what happened and tells {N}, later, that it was well handled.
fails: The customer storms off to complain, and {N} spends the afternoon in the back office explaining.
fails: {N} gets through the shift with shaking hands, and cries in the staff toilet at the break.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- shop assistant, settled (tenure 2-7): calls BR (cycle s4)

== the keys and the alarm code
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-75
calls: BR
holds: shop assistant
tenure: 2-7
rate: 1
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, the regulars
earth: after a few years on the shop floor you are offered the keys: opening up at six on Sundays, the alarm code and the float
times: Many shop workers who stay a few years, once, when the manager trusts them with opening up; many leave retail sooner (estimate)
scene: The manager slides a set of keys across the stockroom table. Sunday openings, the alarm code, the float in the safe: {N} has been on the shop floor long enough to be trusted with them.
scene W: A keyholder is the one who arrives first, leaves last, and whose name goes on the form when anything goes missing. {N} takes that seriously.
scene U: With the keys comes the back office: the ordering screen, the rota, the week's figures. {N} has always wanted to know how the place really runs.
scene B: The keys come with a little more an hour, and with them the first step toward running a shop. {N} wonders what to ask for in return.
scene R: Six o'clock on a Sunday. Every Sunday. {N} thinks of the long lie-ins and the late Saturday nights that would go with it.
scene G: The shop has stood on this corner since before {N} was born, and the old regulars come in for the paper at seven. Somebody has to be there to open the door for them.
- W | take the keys, learn the alarm and the safe, and never be late | v: conformity, security | grants: trusted with the keys | chance: 80
- U | use the back-office login to read what everyone else is paid | v: self-direction (curiosity), power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: snooping in staff records; backfire: the system logs who looked, and the keys go back | tech: computer | chance: 75
- B | ask for the extra pay in writing before touching the keys | v: power, security | chance: 75
- R | say no: Sunday is the one morning that belongs to nobody but you | v: hedonism, self-direction | act: say no, because Sunday is the one morning that is still their own | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 92
- G | take the keys, and open up for the old regulars at seven every Sunday | habit | v: benevolence, tradition | grants: trusted with the keys | self_control: + | chance: 75
- W>G | agree, and ask for the older colleague to share the Sundays by turns | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 70
- U>W | read the keyholder's handbook twice, and pin a checklist inside the door | v: conformity, achievement | chance: 85
- B>U | ask the area manager to put you on the stock course, for the extra grade | door | v: achievement | act: ask the area manager for a place on the stock course, for the extra grade | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- R>B | take the keys, and ask for the supervisor's job while they still need you | v: power, achievement | act: take the keys, and ask for the supervisor's job while the shop still needs them | title: shift manager | chance: 55
- G>R | say yes, and make the slow Sunday mornings your own, radio on | v: hedonism | chance: 75
works: The first month of Sundays goes without a hitch, and the manager starts leaving {N} the notes for the week.
works: {N} turns the key at six, the alarm goes quiet, and for an hour the shop feels like {Ns} own.
fails: The alarm goes off on the first Sunday, the code will not stop it, and the police arrive before the manager does.
fails: Two months in, the float is short one Sunday, and it is {Ns} name on the form.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- shop assistant, senior (tenure 8-60): calls WB (cycle s1)

== the owner wants to retire
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 22-80
calls: WB
holds: shop assistant
tenure: 8-60
rate: .5
once: yes
tone: mixed
life: work, money, community
horizon: years
roles: boss, partner, the regulars, a bank manager
earth: you have worked in the same shop for years, and the owner, who is retiring, asks whether you want to take it on before it closes
times: Rare: perhaps 1 in 10 long-serving staff in a small shop, once; most small shops close or are sold when the owner retires (estimate)
scene: {boss} has run the shop for thirty years, and {N} has stood behind its counter for more than ten of them. Over a cup of tea in the back, {boss} says it is time to retire, and asks whether {N} wants to take it on, or whether the shutters come down in the spring.
scene W: The shop has a lease, suppliers, accounts and twenty years of habits. Taking it on would mean all of them, done properly, every single day.
scene U: {N} has seen the figures over {Ns} shoulder for years: a good December, a thin February, and rent that rises every spring. On paper, with some changes, it could work.
scene B: Ten years of someone else's shop, and now the chance of one of {Ns} own. {N} starts working out what it would cost, and what it could earn.
scene R: {N} feels a rush at the idea, then a sinking: no more going home at six, no more free weekends, all of it riding on one person.
scene G: The old lady at number nine comes in for her bread at eight every morning; the boys from the estate buy their sweets here, as their fathers did. {N} cannot picture the street without the shop.
- W | agree to run it for the owner, as manager, until a buyer is found | v: security, conformity | chance: 75
- U | ask for a full year's figures and a month to think before answering | v: security, self-direction | chance: 90
- B | haggle the price down hard, then take the shop on | binds | v: power, achievement | title: shop owner | requires: savings | without: means | chance: 50
