- R>U | throw yourself into the school play, and learn stagecraft as you go | v: stimulation, achievement | grants: stagecraft | chance: 70
- G>B | wait your turn, as the old staff did, and let the years carry you up | v: tradition, achievement | act: wait their turn, as the old staff did, and let the years carry them up | chance: 55
works: The choice turns out well, and by the summer {N} cannot imagine having chosen otherwise.
works: The head stops {N} in the corridor in June to say how much difference it has made.
fails: The interview goes badly, and {N} goes back to the classroom on Monday with bruised pride.
fails: The extra load comes on top of the marking, and by March {N} is exhausted.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- teacher, senior (tenure 8-60): calls WG (cycle s1)

== a former pupil walks in as the new teacher
tier: everyday
at: school
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 33-75
calls: WG
holds: teacher
tenure: 8-60
rate: .5
once: yes
tone: joy
life: work, school
horizon: years
roles: colleague, boss, the pupils
earth: after many years at the same school, a pupil you once taught comes back as a newly qualified teacher, and joins your department
times: Rare: perhaps 1 in 10 long-serving teachers, once, after ten years or more; some new teachers return to the school they went to (estimate)
scene: The new teacher in {Ns} department is {colleague}, whom {N} taught twelve years ago: the quiet one in the third row, now holding a timetable and asking where the photocopier is.
scene W: {N} is asked to mentor {colleague} through the first year: watch lessons, sign off the standards, write the reports. It is a duty {N} means to do well.
scene U: Twelve years of lesson plans, worksheets, mistakes and fixes sit in {Ns} cupboard. {N} knows exactly which of them are worth handing on.
scene B: The department is being reorganised next year, and a new head will be chosen. {N} notices how much the head likes {colleague}.
scene R: {N} feels a rush of pride so strong it is almost embarrassing, and then a pang: when did it get to be this long?
scene G: Things come round. The young take the places of the old, and some of them come back to the rooms where they once sat.
- W | mentor them properly: watch a lesson every week, and write it all up | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 80
- U | hand over the cupboard of lesson plans, and say which ones failed | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 92
- B | keep your best lessons to yourself; the newcomer may be a rival soon | v: power, security | chance: 85
- R | team-teach a wild project with them, the kind you stopped doing years ago | v: stimulation | chance: 70
- G | leave at the end of the year, and let the young ones have the room | identity | v: tradition (acceptance), self-direction | drops: teacher | chance: 75
- W>U | ask to join the panel that trains the county's new teachers | door | v: benevolence, achievement | chance: 55
- U>B | use twelve years of results to ask for the head of department's job | v: power, achievement | grants: inner circle at work | chance: 45
- B>R | bargain for a four-day week, and spend the fifth on what you always put off | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 55
- R>G | tell them, on the first day, what it meant to see them walk in | v: benevolence | chance: 85
- G>W | give them your old form group, as your own teacher once handed you one | v: tradition | act: give the newcomer their old form group, as their own teacher once handed them one | chance: 65
works: The year goes well: {colleague} finds their feet, and {N} finds something of the old fire.
works: At the end of the year {colleague} gives {N} a card that says, simply, that {N} was the one who made them want to teach.
fails: {colleague} struggles, and {N} cannot tell whether the help is helping or getting in the way.
fails: The year turns awkward: the old roles do not fit the new ones, and the staffroom notices.
